<ContentPage title="Location" subtitle="Coroico and the Yungas — where cloud forest meets civilization.">
  <p>
    BARK is planned for the Coroico area in the Yungas region of Bolivia — a subtropical valley on the eastern
    slopes of the Andes, about 100 km from La Paz.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the Yungas</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Climate</strong> — subtropical at ~1,700m elevation. Warm but not extreme. Year-round growing season. Abundant rainfall.</li>
    <li><strong>Water</strong> — mountain streams, springs, and rainfall provide reliable water sources</li>
    <li><strong>Biodiversity</strong> — one of the most biodiverse regions on Earth. Extraordinary potential for permaculture and food forestry.</li>
    <li><strong>Proximity to La Paz</strong> — 3-4 hours by road. Access to international airport, markets, government, medical facilities.</li>
    <li><strong>Affordability</strong> — land costs are a fraction of comparable locations in North America or Europe</li>
    <li><strong>Legal framework</strong> — Bolivia recognizes cooperatives and community organizations. Foreign land ownership has restrictions but cooperative structures work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why Bolivia</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Geopolitical distance</strong> — far from major conflict zones and power projection theaters</li>
    <li><strong>Resource sovereignty</strong> — Bolivia has lithium, gas, minerals, water, and agricultural land</li>
    <li><strong>Cultural fit</strong> — strong traditions of communal organization (ayllu system), cooperative economics, and community self-governance</li>
    <li><strong>Cost of living</strong> — dramatically lower than developed countries, stretching every dollar of investment further</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Coroico Specifically</h2>
  <p>
    Coroico is a small town (~18,000 in the municipality) with existing tourist infrastructure, a mild
    climate, and stunning Yungas cloud forest surroundings. It has road access, electricity, cellular coverage,
    and a community accustomed to international visitors. The surrounding area has available land suitable
    for the community's needs.
  </p>

  <h2>Integration, Not Colonization</h2>
  <p>
    We are guests in this land. BARK's relationship with the local community is built on mutual benefit:
    local employment, shared infrastructure, cultural exchange, economic integration. We bring resources
    and skills; the community brings knowledge, culture, and belonging. This is a partnership.
  </p>

